            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Denver to Paris?</h3>
            <p>January and February offer the lowest fares, typically $550-$900 round-trip in economy. For summer visits, book 45-70 days ahead and consider April or October. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend prices by a meaningful margin.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Denver to Paris?</h3>
            <p>United Airlines operates seasonal nonstop service from Denver to Paris Charles de Gaulle, typically from late spring through early autumn. Flight duration is approximately 9 hours and 30 minutes. During winter months, all itineraries require at least one connection through domestic or European hubs.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a visa to visit Paris from the US?</h3>
            <p>United States citizens do not require a visa for short tourist stays in France. The Schengen Agreement permits visits up to 90 days within any rolling 180-day period. A valid passport with at least three months' validity beyond your planned departure from France is required.</p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Denver to Paris flight?</h3>
            <p>Most full-service carriers include one checked bag up to 50 pounds and one carry-on bag plus a personal item on transatlantic itineraries. Premium cabin passengers and elite status members typically receive additional allowances. Basic economy fares and European low-cost connections may impose separate baggage charges.</p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Charles de Gaulle Airport to central Paris?</h3>
            <p>The RER B train offers the most economical connection, reaching central Paris in 35-45 minutes for approximately €11.80. The RoissyBus express reaches Opéra in 60-75 minutes for €16.20. Official taxis charge flat rates of €50-€62 to central Paris. Pre-booked private transfers provide door-to-door convenience at variable cost.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Paris, or do I need euros?</h3>
            <p>France uses the euro exclusively, and US dollars are not accepted. ATMs are abundant throughout Paris, and credit cards work at most establishments. Carrying some cash remains advisable for smaller cafés, bakeries, and vendors at open-air markets that may not process cards.</p>

            <h3>Q7: Is Paris safe for American tourists?</h3>
            <p>Paris is generally very safe for visitors, though petty theft and pickpocketing occur in tourist-heavy areas like the Eiffel Tower, Louvre, and Metro lines. Secure valuables, remain aware in crowded spaces, and avoid engaging with street scammers. Violent crime against tourists is rare, and police presence is substantial in central arrondissements.</p>

            <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in Paris for first-time visitors?</h3>
            <p>The 4th, 5th, and 6th arrondissements (Marais, Latin Quarter, Saint-Germain) offer ideal central locations with excellent Metro access, historic atmosphere, and abundant dining. The 7th arrondissement places you near the Eiffel Tower. Montmartre provides artistic charm with hilltop views. Choose based on your sightseeing priorities and budget.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Denver International for my Paris flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from Denver International. During peak summer travel or ski season holidays, consider arriving 3.5 hours early. TSA Pre-Check and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times at Denver's efficient screening checkpoints.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Paris is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Airlines must provide meals and accommodation for qualifying overnight delays depending on cause and duration. For cancellations, EU regulations mandate rebooking or refunds when flying EU-based carriers, and most major airlines extend comparable protections. Contact your airline immediately and maintain comprehensive travel insurance for additional coverage against unforeseen disruptions.</p>



